00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.279
So let's start easy-- stateless.

00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:23.080
I expect most people
in the audience

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.930
to know exactly what
I'm going to do here.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.870
So the meme creator
service is a great example

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.100
of a stateless app.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:34.060
All it does is take in
a few simple parameters

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.615
and return a JPEG.

00:04:35.615 --> 00:04:37.450
It's 100% stateless
and configless.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.450
There's one API, a get
with URL parameters.

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:41.580
No dependencies.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.140
It's a single, standalone
Docker container.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.470
So here's the things we need
to run this stateless service.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.450
The first is the
concept of a pod.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.790
Again, this is more
101, but we had

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.762
to learn this to
go further, right?

00:04:54.762 --> 00:04:57.095
So you have a group or one
or more containers that share

00:04:57.095 --> 00:04:59.900
a local host and are
always scheduled together.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:01.120
They all share the same IP.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:03.550
They live and die together.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:04.750
Then we have deployments.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.960
And basically, this says,
I want so many pods to run,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.030
what the pods need,
and the deployment

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:13.120
takes care of the rest
using the replica set.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.710
And we have a service.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:17.350
And because we can have
arbitrary number of pods,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:19.990
we need a stable endpoint to
do service discovery and load

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:20.935
balancing.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.680
So if you look at the meme
creator service and deployment,

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.470
it looks something like this.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.130
You have a bunch of
pods with a random ID.

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.350
And let's say I
want three of them.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.750
When I have a request come
into the meme creator service,

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:40.350
it'll round robin load
balance to an available pod.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:41.310
That's it.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:47.270
If a pod dies, it'll be replaced
by a new one with a new ID.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.510
We don't regenerate pods.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:50.950
And so that's really basic.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.112
This is the Hello
World of Kubernetes.

00:05:53.112 --> 00:05:54.570
Let's just go
through a quick demo.

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.310
And I'll also record my
screen, just in case.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.040
So here we have--

00:06:09.277 --> 00:06:10.860
I'm going to run a
deployment with two

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.290
replicas with the NGINX
image and open up port 80.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.070
So our deployment is created.

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.150
Very easy.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.417
What does that look like?

00:06:20.417 --> 00:06:21.250
Something like this.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.375
Now, this is the full YAML.

00:06:22.375 --> 00:06:25.850
Of course, in real life,
you would find a lot less.

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:29.170
I can see that I have
two available replicas.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:32.090
I have two replicas in my spec.

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.400
Port 80's open.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.260
Image is NGINX.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.010
Exactly what we specified
in that command.

00:06:40.104 --> 00:06:41.520
Now what I'm going
to do is expose

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.220
that using a load balancer.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:47.230
So I'm going to go kubectl
expose and the nginx-deployment

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:48.330
and type load balancer.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:50.280
This, of course, as
most of you know,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.190
will spin up a Google
Cloud TCP load balancer.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.944
And TCP load balancers
are not global.

00:06:54.944 --> 00:06:55.860
They're very regional.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.680
And you can see that
the nginx-deployment

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.000
has been created.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.300
But the external IP
is still pending.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.370
So in a few seconds, we'll
get that external IP.

00:07:15.356 --> 00:07:16.354
That's good.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:21.712
The fun waiting game.

00:07:21.712 --> 00:07:23.170
We're going to be
doing this a lot.

00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:35.170
It's not even exciting.

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:36.378
I'm just going to keep going.

00:07:36.378 --> 00:07:38.630
Can we switch back
to the slides?

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.340
I'll show you it later.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:41.210
All right, cool.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.550
So then IP would have
come and we would have

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:45.560
seen Hello World from NGINX.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:46.580
Very exciting.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:47.230
Not really.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:48.980
So let's go to something
a little bit more

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:50.840
exciting and a little
bit more complicated.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:52.820
So stateful, right?

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:55.320
Containers can have state.

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.500
And the best example of
this is MongoDB database.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.407
We're not running this in GCE.

00:08:00.407 --> 00:08:01.990
We're not running
this somewhere else.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:05.350
We're running it in Kubernetes.

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:06.510
But there's problems.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.370
There are serious
problems with this,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.520
especially if you try to do it
with a deployment and replica

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.419
sets.

00:08:13.419 --> 00:08:15.960
Among MongoDB replica set, not
to be confused with Kubernetes

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.290
replica sets, are
complicated to set up

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.390
and are complicated
to keep running.

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.700
Every single instruction
on MongoDB's website

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:25.890
requires you to
log into a machine,

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.860
connect to the mongod client,
do some commands here and there,

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:31.980
set up your rs.conf, do
all this complicated stuff.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.250
Turns out in Kubernetes land,
SSHing is not something you do,

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.240
right?

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.460
It requires high
availability and performance.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.770
A database can't go down.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.032
It's very hard for it to
recover, things like that.

00:08:43.032 --> 00:08:44.490
And it has to have
high performance

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.642
SSDs, things like that.

00:08:46.642 --> 00:08:48.850
Of course, obviously, needs
to store persistent data,

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.460
otherwise, it's not
called a database.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.340
But MongoDB is not cloud native.

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.610
A lot of databases can kind of
deal with node failure, deal

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.830
with new member addition,
deal with all those kind

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:02.160
of non-cloud stuff.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.910
I mean, cloud stuff very easily.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:05.550
MongoDB, sadly cannot.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.280
It really depends on hosts.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.980
And it can't be load
balanced, at least not easily.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:13.650
You should really connect to
all members of the replica set.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.660
You can't connect to a
load balancer service

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.390
and have the requests
pushed through like a proxy.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:22.134
You can, but it's
not native, again.

00:09:22.134 --> 00:09:24.800
So what we're going to do is use
a few things that were released

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.370
in the latest version
of Kubernetes,

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.227
especially something
called stateful sets.

00:09:30.227 --> 00:09:32.060
So the first thing we're
going to talk about

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:33.544
are persistent volumes.

00:09:33.544 --> 00:09:35.210
These are data volumes
that persist even

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.910
if the pod restarts or moves.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.850
Then we have sidecar
containers, that it's basically

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:43.855
a container that helps the
primary container do its job.

00:09:43.855 --> 00:09:47.640
So we'll take a look at why
we need that with MongoDB.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:50.660
Then we have stateful
sets, which are basically

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.460
a set of pods that can
scale horizontally,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.870
just like replica sets.

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:57.140
But each pod maintains a
consistent network and disk

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:58.010
identity.

00:09:58.010 --> 00:10:01.160
So this means that
if you lose pod two,

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.140
you'll get pod two back.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:06.360
And it'll attach the same
disk that it had previously,

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.182
which is very important
for databases.

00:10:08.182 --> 00:10:09.890
And finally, the last
piece of the puzzle

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:11.840
is a headless service,
which is a service that

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:13.760
does no load
balancing, but instead,

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.820
exposes the pods directly
with their own DNS names.

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:21.580
So what we have here
is our headless service

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:24.360
and our MongoDB stateful set.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.510
The first thing that happens
is if a volume does not

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.800
exist for that pod, it'll
provision a volume for you.

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.530
Then it'll spin up the pod
and the mongod instance

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:37.590
will store its data
in that volume.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.276
And then we say, I
want three of these,

00:10:39.276 --> 00:10:40.650
so it does three
of these for us.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:49.400
Now, what happens is the
configuration sidecar

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.210
is a little Node.js process
that actually has a Kubernetes

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:54.890
API built into it.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.990
So ill talk to the
headless service

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.140
to discover the other members
of the MongoDB replica set.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:01.191
Now, this is really cool.

00:11:01.191 --> 00:11:03.440
This means we don't have to
actually configure MongoDB

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.580
manually.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:07.970
Instead, we just had the sidecar
autoconfigure our cluster

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.455
for us.

00:11:09.455 --> 00:11:11.830
So once it talks and figures
out the rest of the members,

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:16.790
it'll create the replica
set for us automatically.

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:20.620
And when our app wants to talk
to the MongoDB replica set,

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:24.850
it bypasses the headless service
and talks directly to the pods.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:28.860
So the first pod is called
mongo-0.mongo, mongo-1.mongo,

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:31.030
and mongo-2.mongo.

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:33.490
We're not talking to
the Mongo service,

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:35.440
we're talking to each
pod directly using

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:37.599
the headless service.

00:11:37.599 --> 00:11:38.890
All right, can you switch back?

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.020
So at this point,
we have our IP.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:48.391
We can obviously go there, but
you can take my word for it,

00:11:48.391 --> 00:11:48.890
it works.

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.160
Here you go.

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:52.990
