00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.704
So the first thing I'm
doing is creating this thing

00:12:11.704 --> 00:12:13.450
called a storage class.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:17.050
And a storage class is
not technically necessary,

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:19.290
but I like it because I can
control what kind of disk

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.410
gets provisioned.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:21.940
And I really want an SSD.

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:24.250
So I made a new source
class called fast

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:27.820
that provision Kubernetes.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.630
It makes Kubernetes aware
of the GCE persistent disk

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:38.200
and makes you able to provision
SSDs on the fly, which is nice.

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:40.870
And now I'm actually going to
create the MongoDB service.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:45.410
And here's what happens.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:49.840
You can see that we have
our mongo-0 spinning up.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.180
But we don't have the
other two replicas.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:55.090
This is because the stateful
set actually spins up

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:57.244
the pods one at a time.

00:12:57.244 --> 00:12:58.660
And if you think
about it, this is

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:01.270
really important for most
traditional databases,

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:04.030
because the replica set
has to be created first

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:07.240
before a member can join.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.049
And so what this
ensures is that you

00:13:09.049 --> 00:13:11.590
don't have two people thinking
that they're both the primary,

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.506
and then you have to do
reelections and things

00:13:13.506 --> 00:13:14.170
like that.

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:16.300
Instead, we wait till
the first container

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.820
is created before
starting the next one,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.122
and before starting
the next one.

00:13:21.122 --> 00:13:23.080
Obviously, the first step
is to actually create

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:24.227
those persistent disks.

00:13:24.227 --> 00:13:26.560
So you can see that we have
three persistent disks being

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:27.520
created already.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:29.680
And they're bound to our nodes.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.020
So we have storage
mongo-0, storage mongo-1,

00:13:32.020 --> 00:13:34.580
and storage mongo-2.

00:13:34.580 --> 00:13:38.387
Now, the other thing to notice
is the Mongo service itself.

00:13:38.387 --> 00:13:40.470
So we have our nginx-deployment
on the very bottom

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.740
that has a cluster IP
and an external IP.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:47.940
The Mongo service has
a none for cluster IP.

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:50.810
Now, you might think that's
kind of weird, and it is.

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.740
It's because it's
a headless service.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.210
There is no load balancer
for the Mongo service.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:56.100
We don't need it.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.350
Instead, it exposes the
pods, mongo-0, mongo-1,

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:02.339
and mongo-2 directly
to our application.

