00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:03.770
Because it's hard, right.

00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:05.320
Yeah, multi-region is hard.

00:16:05.320 --> 00:16:06.740
Multi-cloud is hard.

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:09.500
But it's 2017 and we
can do better than this.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:13.050
And Kubernetes is really
trying to make it easier,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:13.884
but it's still hard.

00:16:13.884 --> 00:16:14.550
It's still hard.

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:15.380
Don't get me wrong.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:17.180
But we have to do this.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:19.610
Otherwise, when a cloud provider
goes down for any reason,

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:23.390
and we all do, things
just break, right?

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:26.870
I saw a great blog post
saying, because of Kubernetes,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.090
with the S3 outage,
they failed over just

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:31.730
seamlessly without
any sort of downtime,

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.760
because they had
this redundancy.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.260
And this happens
to everyone, right?

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.700
It's not just AWS or Google.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:38.970
Everyone has downtime.

00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:42.794
So with Kubernetes, you can
really hedge against that.

00:16:42.794 --> 00:16:44.460
So we're going to
talk about two things.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.796
The first thing is the
federation control plan.

00:16:46.796 --> 00:16:48.170
And it's basically
a service that

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:51.440
