00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:53.940
Easy.

00:23:53.940 --> 00:23:56.590
I don't have to care.

00:23:56.590 --> 00:23:57.550
So now we're done.

00:23:57.550 --> 00:24:03.109
We have set up six federated
clusters around the world.

00:24:03.109 --> 00:24:05.400
We just set up six federated
clusters around the world,

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:05.900
there we go.

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:07.736
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:09.220 --> 00:24:11.030
All right, can we switch
back to the demo--

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.730
to the slides?

00:24:13.730 --> 00:24:16.460
So you might be
wondering why didn't I

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:19.160
just make a really
big cluster that

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:20.469
just spanned the whole world?

00:24:20.469 --> 00:24:22.010
Why did I go with
the second one that

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:25.790
looks a lot more complicated
and looks like Christmas?

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:27.770
It's because reasons.

00:24:27.770 --> 00:24:31.310
And the reasons are, what
if that VM on the left

00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:34.920
is in Australia and the
other VM is in Singapore?

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:36.920
Well, they probably
shouldn't talk to each other

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:40.350
if there's a closer one, right?

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.360
And what happens
if this just dies?

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:43.920
What if the cluster dies?

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:47.430
All services around the
world just die, right?

00:24:47.430 --> 00:24:50.520
With the second option, even
if the global control plane

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.230
goes down, all
the local clusters

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:54.570
are still up and running,
functional, fully functional,

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:55.070
right?

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:58.550
You just can't address
them at the global scale.

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:01.170
And if little clusters
go down, it's OK.

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:02.820
Traffic will reroute
intelligently

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.820
to available clusters.

00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:07.200
How?

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:09.220
Something like this.

00:25:09.220 --> 00:25:12.570
So if service A, the app
wants to connect to service A,

00:25:12.570 --> 00:25:15.270
obviously it'll just connect
to the one in the cluster.

00:25:15.270 --> 00:25:17.350
But if it wants to
connect to service C,

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:18.725
it will say, hey,
there's nothing

00:25:18.725 --> 00:25:20.590
available in the cluster.

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:23.200
Let me go to another cluster.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:24.090
That's pretty cool.

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.590
This means that you don't have
to have your services running

00:25:26.590 --> 00:25:30.910
in every cluster if it's
not a highly used service.

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:33.642
In fact, we'll talk
about something like that

00:25:33.642 --> 00:25:34.600
in this part right now.

00:25:37.400 --> 00:25:42.775
Because if you go to
mywebsite.com, from there,

00:25:42.775 --> 00:25:45.940
you probably want to go to
Sao Paulo data center, not

00:25:45.940 --> 00:25:47.470
somewhere else, right?

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:49.390
In fact, your users
around the world

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:52.480
probably wanted to go to the
data center closest to them.

00:25:52.480 --> 00:25:54.480
But they all want to just
type in mywebsite.com.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:57.670
It's super annoying when I
have to type in mywebsite.uk,

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:02.080
or my website dot
whatever, right?

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:03.670
I just want to type
in mywebsite.com

00:26:03.670 --> 00:26:06.750
and get the fastest result.

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:08.280
Turns out we have this.

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:10.110
This is an actual map--

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:11.940
actual map.

00:26:11.940 --> 00:26:13.690
Those black points are
actually our points

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.990
of presences around
the world, which means

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:20.080
that if you connect
to one of these,

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:25.870
you're routed automatically
using SDN networking, right?

00:26:25.870 --> 00:26:29.560
So we have an anycast system
to the closest data center.

00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:32.590
What this means is every single
one of these points of presence

00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:35.320
broadcasts the same IP address.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.270
And the user connects to the
closest one automatically.

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:40.690
This means you're not worrying
about DNS configuration

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:41.606
or anything like that.

00:26:41.606 --> 00:26:43.330
You just say, I have one IP.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:46.420
Google, you take
care of the rest.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:49.350
And so how do you do that?

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.830
Again, federated ingress.

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:56.600
This is a native Kubernetes
L7 load balancer and router.

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:59.740
And it's federated, which means
that you get one IP address

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:01.726
for all of your clusters.

00:27:01.726 --> 00:27:03.850
And the way you do this on
Google Cloud, of course,

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:06.070
is the HTTPS load balancer.

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:09.430
Now, this is our global
L7 load balancing solution

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:11.740
with a single IP
address, SSL offload,

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:16.925
and in alpha, IP v-6 support,
which is really, really good.

00:27:16.925 --> 00:27:18.550
So let's take a look
at how that works.

