Boom.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.930
Then I'm going to
the meme creator.

00:27:45.696 --> 00:27:47.540
Sweet.

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:49.407
Then I'm going to do--

00:27:49.407 --> 00:27:50.240
what else do I have?

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:51.260
I don't even know.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:54.415
Let's see, I'm going
to do the website.

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.830
So this is our web front
end, and we're good to go.

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:02.519
Now, I'm also going to
deploy our MongoDB stack.

00:28:02.519 --> 00:28:04.060
And because it's
only in one cluster,

00:28:04.060 --> 00:28:05.617
it's a little bit different.

00:28:12.590 --> 00:28:14.590
And I'm going to deploy
this little thing called

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.930
database helper, which I
will talk about in a second.

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:32.599
Now, if I do a make
get service, I'm

00:28:32.599 --> 00:28:34.390
just going to loop
through all the clusters

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.220
and get all the services.

00:28:36.220 --> 00:28:38.920
So you can see that with
just those commands,

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:41.020
it basically federated
all of these tasks

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:42.850
into all six clusters, right?

00:28:42.850 --> 00:28:44.980
I ran it on the
worldscalemicroservices

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:45.910
context.

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:48.280
And because that's a
virtual federated context,

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:52.060
it automatically distributed all
the pods into all the clusters.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:56.450
I didn't have to log
into each one and deploy.

00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:59.840
So of course, what we want
to do now is the ingress.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:00.640
So let's do that.

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:09.350
OK.

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:12.590
So can we go back to the
slides for one second?

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:13.850
Perfect, thank you.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:17.420
This is all I need
to do for ingress.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:21.110
And this creates that
global load balancer.

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:24.537
You can see that looks really
simple, except for one line.

00:29:24.537 --> 00:29:26.787
That weird line that says
kubernetes.io/ingress.global

00:29:26.787 --> 00:29:31.220
static ip name
kubernetes-ingress.

00:29:31.220 --> 00:29:33.340
So this is because we
need to actually give it

00:29:33.340 --> 00:29:35.040
that one static IP address.

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:37.370
Otherwise, it'll all
try to bring its own.

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:39.170
And this was really
simple to do.

00:29:39.170 --> 00:29:42.180
Can we switch back to my laptop?

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.430
All I had to do was this.

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:45.320
Where is it?

00:29:45.320 --> 00:29:48.700
Let's see, ingress,
which is somewhere here.

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:52.950
gcloud compute addresses create
kubernetes english --global.

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:55.980
This will create a global IP
address for me on Google Cloud.

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:58.350
Once I do that, I can use
this Kubernetes ingress

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.000
in my Kubernetes natively.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.490
I don't have to actually go
ahead and click any buttons

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:04.980
on the UI or anything like that.

00:30:04.980 --> 00:30:08.070
So now, if we go back
to our networking page,

00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:10.637
right here, this
networking page,

00:30:10.637 --> 00:30:12.220
you can see that I
