00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:44.960
So if I run make.ingress, you
can see that all of my clusters

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:51.320
all have the same IP address for
the service all over the world.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:55.418
And it's probably still
spinning up, but let's try it.

00:30:55.418 --> 00:30:56.420
It's still spinning up.

00:30:56.420 --> 00:30:59.250
That's OK.

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:01.004
Now, we'll wait for that.

00:31:01.004 --> 00:31:03.420
And when that's going up, we'll
just go back to the slides

00:31:03.420 --> 00:31:09.240
real fast and talk
about configuration.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:10.890
Because why not
easy coding, right?

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:12.600
Why is there no term
called easy coding?

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:16.530
These are interesting.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:18.320
The regional buckets.

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:22.101
For a lot of people, they want
their data to be regional.

00:31:22.101 --> 00:31:24.350
They don't want it to be
distributed around the world.

00:31:24.350 --> 00:31:26.660
Sure, you've got a
little bit less uptime,

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:30.782
but you want it to
be cheaper, maybe.

00:31:30.782 --> 00:31:31.490
Mostly for memes.

00:31:31.490 --> 00:31:32.750
Who wants to pay for memes?

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:36.800
And you want it to be
regional so it's faster.

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:37.665
But where?

00:31:37.665 --> 00:31:40.110
How do you know which
bucket to write to, right?

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:43.357
US Central, Asia
East, Mars-- oh, wait.

00:31:43.357 --> 00:31:43.940
That's a leak.

00:31:43.940 --> 00:31:44.755
Sorry.

00:31:44.755 --> 00:31:45.255
Nope.

00:31:45.255 --> 00:31:48.200
Mars isn't-- is a rumor.

00:31:48.200 --> 00:31:50.450
We don't have one of those.

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:52.132
You could do
something like this.

00:31:52.132 --> 00:31:53.840
You can get the current
zone dynamically,

00:31:53.840 --> 00:31:56.870
because GCE will report what
data center it's running in.

00:31:56.870 --> 00:31:59.010
And then you have this
huge mapping table saying,

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:02.370
if I'm running in US West
1A, then use bucket 1.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:04.280
And oh my gosh.

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:06.290
Every time you
add something new,

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:09.940
let's say you want to add
S3 into this, well, OK.

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:11.520
Got to go edit it, right?

00:32:11.520 --> 00:32:12.799
And that sucks.

00:32:12.799 --> 00:32:14.090
What if you could just do this?

00:32:14.090 --> 00:32:16.040
Just use the
environment variable

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:18.250
and automatically get it.

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:20.000
That sounds good to me.

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.480
So what we're going to
do is use a config map

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:24.340
to store that information.

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:26.590
So this is basically some
configuration information

00:32:26.590 --> 00:32:28.930
that's exposed to a container,
either as an environment

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.850
variable or a file.

00:32:30.850 --> 00:32:33.924
And then a secret is basically
a config map, but obfuscated.

00:32:33.924 --> 00:32:35.590
There's a lot of
debate in the community

00:32:35.590 --> 00:32:36.940
over it's not encrypted.

00:32:36.940 --> 00:32:37.960
Yes, it's not encrypted.

00:32:37.960 --> 00:32:40.960
You can use Vault. Kelsey
has a great tutorial on how

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:43.212
you can use Vault to make
truly encrypted secrets.

00:32:43.212 --> 00:32:45.670
But yeah, right now the default
thing is just obfuscate it.

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:48.000
Just to keep in mind.

00:32:48.000 --> 00:32:50.810
All right, so let's
go back to the demo.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:52.610
At this point, I hope it's up.

00:32:52.610 --> 00:32:54.330
Let's see.

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:55.480
There we go.

00:32:55.480 --> 00:32:56.460
Awesome.

00:32:56.460 --> 00:32:58.780
So here is my thing.

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:00.630
So you can see that
it's detecting that it's

00:33:00.630 --> 00:33:02.770
