00:36:17.740 --> 00:36:19.520
Here we go.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:21.580
Got a little crash loop.

00:36:21.580 --> 00:36:24.750
Oh, interesting.

00:36:24.750 --> 00:36:27.670
So our image store
service is creating.

00:36:27.670 --> 00:36:32.785
I have a bad feeling I can't
access Docker help from here.

00:36:32.785 --> 00:36:34.700
Huh, interesting.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:37.370
So basically, our
MongoDB pod is not

00:36:37.370 --> 00:36:39.330
creating, which is
really interesting.

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:40.580
It's dependent on Docker help.

00:36:40.580 --> 00:36:41.540
Everything else is not.

00:36:41.540 --> 00:36:43.137
So I wonder what's going on.

00:36:43.137 --> 00:36:45.220
And this is why-- this is
crash loop back offing--

00:36:45.220 --> 00:36:46.760
because the database
helper is trying

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:48.135
to connect to our
MongoDB replica

00:36:48.135 --> 00:36:49.610
set which is not existing.

00:36:49.610 --> 00:36:51.440
That's OK.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:53.690
So we can see your image
store service is creating.

00:36:53.690 --> 00:36:55.520
The nice thing
about microservices

00:36:55.520 --> 00:37:00.880
is that they can
fail with some grace.

00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:02.120
Let's refresh that.

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:02.900
Here we go.

00:37:02.900 --> 00:37:04.566
You can see that
because the image store

00:37:04.566 --> 00:37:09.000
service is up and running,
those requests got created.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.880
Man, come on, guys.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:12.510
Who is that?

00:37:20.993 --> 00:37:24.527
The cloud is angry at you.

00:37:24.527 --> 00:37:26.610
And if you go to this
website, you can make memes.

00:37:26.610 --> 00:37:27.535
Sure, go ahead.

00:37:27.535 --> 00:37:28.600
Just do it.

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:33.700
But because MongoDB is down,
the searching will not work.

00:37:33.700 --> 00:37:36.850
The interesting thing is all
of these memes, once Mongo

00:37:36.850 --> 00:37:39.270
comes back up, will be saved.

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:41.325
And we'll talk more
about that in a second.

00:37:41.325 --> 00:37:42.700
But around the
world, these would

00:37:42.700 --> 00:37:46.410
be stored in different buckets.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:50.409
And here, if we refresh
our bucket, here we go.

00:37:50.409 --> 00:37:51.700
Bunch of people are make memes.

00:37:51.700 --> 00:37:53.700
If anyone puts anything
inappropriate-- oh,

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:54.658
I'm not even going try.

00:37:54.658 --> 00:37:55.735
All right.

00:37:55.735 --> 00:37:57.700
We're going to
close the website.

00:37:57.700 --> 00:37:58.630
There we go.

00:37:58.630 --> 00:37:59.650
Not tempted.

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:03.950
All right, so if we can go
back to the slides, please?

00:38:03.950 --> 00:38:04.555
Cool.

00:38:04.555 --> 00:38:05.680
So this is the other stuff.

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:07.360
Let's go back.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:09.610
And this is like, I think,
the most important part

00:38:09.610 --> 00:38:10.360
of the talk.

00:38:10.360 --> 00:38:11.470
Everything else is great.

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:15.080
It's really like, wow,
we did stuff, right?

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:19.010
But this took me, basically, all
of January to make by myself.

00:38:19.010 --> 00:38:20.000
All six microservices.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.855
And I learned a lot about
building microservices.

00:38:24.855 --> 00:38:26.480
The first thing you
have to think about

00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:29.060
is this-- sync versus async.

00:38:29.060 --> 00:38:31.250
So synchronous
point-to-point communication

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:32.210
is pretty easy to do.

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:33.920
And I think Kubernetes
really makes

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:37.460
it easy to do more
than just normal.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:40.040
If you have your app and it
wants to talk to a service,

00:38:40.040 --> 00:38:42.350
it just makes a
request to the service.

00:38:42.350 --> 00:38:45.530
And that automatically load
balances to an available pod,

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:47.084
and the pod gives it back.

00:38:47.084 --> 00:38:48.500
It doesn't go
through the service.

00:38:48.500 --> 00:38:49.250
I don't know why I did that.

00:38:49.250 --> 00:38:50.416
It goes directly to the pod.

00:38:50.416 --> 00:38:52.829
But it's pretty easy, right?

00:38:52.829 --> 00:38:55.370
I don't have to worry about how
many pods i have or anything.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:38:57.170
I just hit the service
and we're good to go.

00:38:57.170 --> 00:38:58.628
This can be done
with the REST API.

00:38:58.628 --> 00:39:01.980
It can go with gRPC, or
any other RPC framework.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:03.770
Either way, like
service discovery

00:39:03.770 --> 00:39:06.340
is basically free, right?

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:08.421
And it feels native
to Kubernetes.

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:13.050
Here I would have
three tweets that say,

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:16.287
like, you can't do
microservices with sync.

00:39:16.287 --> 00:39:18.370
But legal wouldn't let me
put tweets on my slides,

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:20.610
so it's blank.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:22.980
But just imagine
the tweets there.

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:25.950
Angry people saying,
you have to have async,

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:28.440
otherwise it's just a
distributed monolith.

00:39:28.440 --> 00:39:33.990
And I kind of agree and
kind of disagree with that.

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:36.010
This is what an async
kind of looks like.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:39.300
Your pod puts something
into a work queue.

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:41.556
The other pods just kind of
read from that work queue,

00:39:41.556 --> 00:39:42.930
finish the work,
and maybe put it

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:46.185
into another queue for
the first pod to consume.

00:39:49.647 --> 00:39:51.230
And so if you look
at our architecture

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:57.780
for creating a new meme, I feel
that these two pieces really

00:39:57.780 --> 00:40:01.260
fit the async nature.

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:01.860
Why?

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:05.730
Because if I say create a meme,
enter, and nothing comes out,

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:06.600
it's broken.

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:08.874
It doesn't matter if it
comes out in like 10 seconds.

00:40:08.874 --> 00:40:09.790
Async does not matter.

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:10.920
It has to be synchronous.

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:12.920
I want it

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:16.870
Through the lifetime of the
request, it has to finish.

00:40:16.870 --> 00:40:20.310
However, storing the
image in the backend

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:21.640
needs to actually finish.

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:23.250
It can't fail, right?

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:25.650
So that needs to
be asynchronous.

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:28.390
And it doesn't matter about
performance as much real, like,

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:30.600
in the lifetime of the request.

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:33.120
Instead, it matters that
it gets done eventually.

00:40:33.120 --> 00:40:35.760
And it gets done consistently.

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:40.410
So what I did is database
helper is actually

00:40:40.410 --> 00:40:43.230
listening to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:40:43.230 --> 00:40:46.290
And this Pub/Sub topic
is getting requests

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:47.250
from image store.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:49.416
And once that request comes
in, that database helper

00:40:49.416 --> 00:40:53.030
is talking directly to
MongoDB and putting it in.

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:55.760
But the other way around, when
you want to query the database,

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:57.280
that's a synchronous service.

00:40:57.280 --> 00:41:00.830
The database helper
exposes a RESTful interface

00:41:00.830 --> 00:41:05.330
and I query MongoDB
through that REST proxy.

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:08.460
Do not expose your
MongoDB to the internet.

00:41:08.460 --> 00:41:11.870
[LAUGHS] Don't do it.

00:41:11.870 --> 00:41:14.910
And use passwords people,
please, please, please.

00:41:14.910 --> 00:41:18.620
So Google Cloud Pub/Sub is an
asynchronous message queue.

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:20.150
Many-to-many communication.

00:41:20.150 --> 00:41:21.320
Fully managed.

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:22.825
It's NoOps, serverless.

00:41:22.825 --> 00:41:24.870
It just scales to millions
of messages instantly.

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:26.994
You don't have to warm it
up or anything like that.

00:41:26.994 --> 00:41:29.030
There's a gRPC API
that's really fast,

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.100
and a REST API that
you can use as well.

00:41:31.100 --> 00:41:33.440
And a bunch of libraries
for about 10 languages.

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:37.822
The normal 10 languages that
we support on Google Cloud.

00:41:37.822 --> 00:41:39.530
So before we go to
the next one, this is,

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:42.252
I think, really important
to understand why

00:41:42.252 --> 00:41:43.460
you want something like this.

00:41:43.460 --> 00:41:44.630
If you've ever had--

00:41:44.630 --> 00:41:45.850
this is why I fed--

00:41:45.850 --> 00:41:47.990
those memes won't
be stored in MongoDB

00:41:47.990 --> 00:41:49.700
now because the
database is down,

00:41:49.700 --> 00:41:51.620
but they'll be
buffered in Pub/Sub,

00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:54.140
waiting for the database
helper to come back up

00:41:54.140 --> 00:41:55.070
and read from them.

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:56.360
And then they'll all come in.

00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:58.610
So even though I had
database downtime,

00:41:58.610 --> 00:42:00.792
it's still safe
sitting in Pub/Sub.

00:42:00.792 --> 00:42:02.750
And of course, I mean,
I work for Google Cloud.

00:42:02.750 --> 00:42:05.180
I recommend using one of
our managed databases.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:07.370
But for a lot of people,
they want to run their own.

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:10.040
So I'm not going to make
any judgments there.

00:42:10.040 --> 00:42:11.420
Only a little bit.

00:42:11.420 --> 00:42:13.970
So the other thing
I want to talk about

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:15.435
are the Stackdriver tools.

00:42:15.435 --> 00:42:18.536
How many of you went to
a Stackdriver talk today?

00:42:18.536 --> 00:42:21.200
OK, fair amount.

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:24.170
I like them a lot, mostly
for something like this.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:25.550
Logging was pretty important.

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:27.720
Now, it's not the most
beautiful logs viewer,

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:30.530
but when you have six
microservices times six

00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:32.691
clusters, that's--

00:42:32.691 --> 00:42:34.440
who can do the math
for me? (WHISPERS) 36.

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:35.600
The answer is 36.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:40.420
36 different services running
around the world, right?

00:42:40.420 --> 00:42:43.460
With Stackdriver Logging, I
was able to take all of those

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:46.010
into one centralized viewpoint.

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:47.660
I can go to any of
my clusters, any

00:42:47.660 --> 00:42:49.034
of the containers
in my clusters,

00:42:49.034 --> 00:42:51.560
and see the logs right there.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:53.240
Something I didn't
think I would use,

00:42:53.240 --> 00:42:55.790
but ended up using because I'm
really bad at writing code,

00:42:55.790 --> 00:42:57.320
was tracing.

00:42:57.320 --> 00:42:59.610
So this is a trace graph.

00:42:59.610 --> 00:43:03.020
This is an actual trace
from one of my requests.

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:05.480
Who can see the really
weird, what in the world

00:43:05.480 --> 00:43:06.850
is going on thing there?

00:43:06.850 --> 00:43:10.070
I was actually not closing
a stream correctly.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:11.540
I was never returning.

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:16.360
And so the process would just
start again after 30 seconds.

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:18.074
And so you can see
that really weird--

00:43:18.074 --> 00:43:19.490
it's kind of blurry
on the screen,

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:23.680
but it goes for like 100
milliseconds, 100 milliseconds,

00:43:23.680 --> 00:43:26.390
few hundred milliseconds,
through 30,000 milliseconds.

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:28.370
And then a few
hundred milliseconds.

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:29.930
So that was clearly
something broken.

00:43:29.930 --> 00:43:31.179
And I could not figure it out.

00:43:31.179 --> 00:43:33.140
But when I saw it
in this trace, it

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:34.790
was like really, really easy.

00:43:34.790 --> 00:43:37.730
And because I write in
Node.js, except meme created,

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:40.850
which is in PHP,
interesting fact,

00:43:40.850 --> 00:43:43.500
it's just Google Cloud
dot, like, start trace.

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:47.065
It's like one line of code to
get all this information, which

00:43:47.065 --> 00:43:47.690
is really cool.

00:43:51.750 --> 00:43:54.460
And then there's so much more.

00:43:54.460 --> 00:43:58.700
Yeah, this is a small list
that me and a coworker

00:43:58.700 --> 00:44:01.010
created over drinks one day.

00:44:01.010 --> 00:44:02.870
And we're like, wow.

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:05.750
There's a lot of stuff to
talk about with microservices.

00:44:05.750 --> 00:44:09.183
Everyone says they're easier
than monoliths, and something

00:44:09.183 --> 00:44:12.082
something something, or
I don't know, whatever.

00:44:12.082 --> 00:44:13.790
But you don't think
about a lot of things

00:44:13.790 --> 00:44:15.260
when you're really doing them.

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:17.050
So people are taking pictures.

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:18.860
This is just a
random list, guys.

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:21.241
This is not real.

00:44:21.241 --> 00:44:21.740
I'm kidding.

00:44:21.740 --> 00:44:22.906
You can take your pictures.

00:44:22.906 --> 00:44:24.030
Take your pictures of this.

00:44:24.030 --> 00:44:29.930
So if you want to learn more
about microservices with us,

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:33.320
me and my coworker, Ray
Tsang, who's another Cloud DA,

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:36.110
we want to create a new blog
post slash video series.

00:44:36.110 --> 00:44:39.230
This is me saying it now so
I'm forced to do it later.

00:44:39.230 --> 00:44:40.700
Follow us on Medium and Twitter.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:44.220
And hopefully, we'll announce
something later this year.

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:47.390
The two of us kind of sat
down and we're like, wow,

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:50.480
there's so many unknown
knowns, known unknowns,

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:52.572
unknown known
knowns, and who knows

00:44:52.572 --> 00:44:54.530
what's going on in this
world of microservices.

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:56.071
A lot of people are
talking about it.

00:44:56.071 --> 00:44:56.990
Let's get practical.

00:44:56.990 --> 00:44:59.420
Let's just try to work
